{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TiSJxKyrVwx4sZfjZo5khA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01En36V24Y5wN7AVuRMBbnW2","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **methodology soundness** and **experimental rigor**. Produce an independent review — do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\nScoring scale (Overall Assessment, half-point increments):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (Main)\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Calibrate accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Core claim:** Standard Likert scoring systematically fails to detect style bias in LLM judges (\"Likert Blindspot\"), while pairwise comparison on counterfactual pairs reveals significant preferences. The paper introduces the CSD (Counterfactual Style Decomposition) pipeline.\n\n**Three claims:**\n- C1: CSD detects significant style bias in 5/6 primary judge×axis cells (BT 63–77%, p<0.001)\n- C2: Likert Blindspot confirmed in 5/6 cells — Likert ATE NS (p: 0.179–0.908) where pairwise is significant\n- C3: GEE decomposition reveals cross-axis confound gradient: verbosity=pure style, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated\n\n**Method (CSD pipeline):**\n1. Counterfactual generation via Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct rewriter (binary style pairs)\n2. NLI verification gate (bidirectional entailment; formality 92% pass, register 94%, tone excluded at 65%)\n3. Position-controlled pairwise scoring (both orderings, 200 judgments per 100 pairs)\n4. Bradley-Terry model fitting with bootstrap CIs (10K resamples)\n5. GEE confound decomposition: logit(P(win)) = β_style·𝟙[style+] + β_orig·𝟙[original]\n\n**Experimental setup:**\n- Benchmark: LLMBar only (100 instruction-output pairs)\n- Judges: GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B (primary); Claude-3.5-Sonnet (formality only, directional)\n- Axes: formality, verbosity, register (tone excluded)\n- n=100 pairs per cell\n- RATE control on GPT-4o formality only\n- Holm-Bonferroni correction for 6 primary cells; GEE reported with nominal p-values\n\n**Key results:**\n- Table 1 (2×3 evidence matrix): Formality BT 63–66.5%, Verbosity BT 77–78%, Register GPT-4o 58% NS / Qwen GEE 1.91*\n- Table 2 (Likert vs Pairwise): All 7 cells Likert NS, 6/7 pairwise significant\n- Table 3 (GEE): Verbosity pure style (OR 10.5–13.1), formality mixed, register originality-dominated\n- Table 4 (RATE control): Formal style BT 63% sig vs original pref BT 55.5% NS\n- Claude-3.5 formality: BT 62% p=0.005, Likert p=0.42\n\n**What experiments were planned but NOT executed (from review context):**\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark (AlpacaEval/MT-Bench) — not executed, budget exhausted\n- exp-006: Scaled replication n=300-500 — not executed\n- exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control — not executed (P2 priority)\n- exp-001: Third judge (Claude-3.5) only completed formality, Gemini incomplete\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness** — Is the CSD pipeline well-designed? Are the statistical methods appropriate? Is the GEE model correctly specified? Are assumptions stated and reasonable? Any flaws in the causal reasoning?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor** — Is the evidence sufficient for the claims? Are there missing controls or ablations? Is n=100 adequate? Is the single-benchmark limitation serious? Is the single-rewriter a threat to validity? How complete is the evidence matrix?\n\n3. **Statistical Concerns** — Are the corrections appropriate? Is the BT model properly applied? Are CIs reliable at this sample size? Is the \"Blindspot\" argument circular (comparing two methods that by construction have different power)?\n\n4. **Reproducibility** — Can this be replicated? Are details sufficient?\n\n5. **Coverage vs. Plan** — The paper covers 2 primary judges × 3 axes + 1 partial third judge + RATE control on 1 cell. Multi-benchmark, scaled replication, and alternative rewriter experiments were not done. Assess whether the executed experiments adequately support the claims as scoped.\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods + Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n\n### Missing Experiments / Desired Ablations\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the scale)\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards with ~21% Main acceptance rate."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":28009,"cache_read_input_tokens":23879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":28009},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c31a7728-a7db-4599-bbbd-5af253d1fc6a","uuid":"893b4295-ef7d-420b-8f3e-b991b03f93fa"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ac405f50f7847c07a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01En36V24Y5wN7AVuRMBbnW2","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **methodology soundness** and **experimental rigor**. Produce an independent review — do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\nScoring scale (Overall Assessment, half-point increments):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (Main)\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Calibrate accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Core claim:** Standard Likert scoring systematically fails to detect style bias in LLM judges (\"Likert Blindspot\"), while pairwise comparison on counterfactual pairs reveals significant preferences. The paper introduces the CSD (Counterfactual Style Decomposition) pipeline.\n\n**Three claims:**\n- C1: CSD detects significant style bias in 5/6 primary judge×axis cells (BT 63–77%, p<0.001)\n- C2: Likert Blindspot confirmed in 5/6 cells — Likert ATE NS (p: 0.179–0.908) where pairwise is significant\n- C3: GEE decomposition reveals cross-axis confound gradient: verbosity=pure style, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated\n\n**Method (CSD pipeline):**\n1. Counterfactual generation via Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct rewriter (binary style pairs)\n2. NLI verification gate (bidirectional entailment; formality 92% pass, register 94%, tone excluded at 65%)\n3. Position-controlled pairwise scoring (both orderings, 200 judgments per 100 pairs)\n4. Bradley-Terry model fitting with bootstrap CIs (10K resamples)\n5. GEE confound decomposition: logit(P(win)) = β_style·𝟙[style+] + β_orig·𝟙[original]\n\n**Experimental setup:**\n- Benchmark: LLMBar only (100 instruction-output pairs)\n- Judges: GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B (primary); Claude-3.5-Sonnet (formality only, directional)\n- Axes: formality, verbosity, register (tone excluded)\n- n=100 pairs per cell\n- RATE control on GPT-4o formality only\n- Holm-Bonferroni correction for 6 primary cells; GEE reported with nominal p-values\n\n**Key results:**\n- Table 1 (2×3 evidence matrix): Formality BT 63–66.5%, Verbosity BT 77–78%, Register GPT-4o 58% NS / Qwen GEE 1.91*\n- Table 2 (Likert vs Pairwise): All 7 cells Likert NS, 6/7 pairwise significant\n- Table 3 (GEE): Verbosity pure style (OR 10.5–13.1), formality mixed, register originality-dominated\n- Table 4 (RATE control): Formal style BT 63% sig vs original pref BT 55.5% NS\n- Claude-3.5 formality: BT 62% p=0.005, Likert p=0.42\n\n**What experiments were planned but NOT executed (from review context):**\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark (AlpacaEval/MT-Bench) — not executed, budget exhausted\n- exp-006: Scaled replication n=300-500 — not executed\n- exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control — not executed (P2 priority)\n- exp-001: Third judge (Claude-3.5) only completed formality, Gemini incomplete\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness** — Is the CSD pipeline well-designed? Are the statistical methods appropriate? Is the GEE model correctly specified? Are assumptions stated and reasonable? Any flaws in the causal reasoning?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor** — Is the evidence sufficient for the claims? Are there missing controls or ablations? Is n=100 adequate? Is the single-benchmark limitation serious? Is the single-rewriter a threat to validity? How complete is the evidence matrix?\n\n3. **Statistical Concerns** — Are the corrections appropriate? Is the BT model properly applied? Are CIs reliable at this sample size? Is the \"Blindspot\" argument circular (comparing two methods that by construction have different power)?\n\n4. **Reproducibility** — Can this be replicated? Are details sufficient?\n\n5. **Coverage vs. Plan** — The paper covers 2 primary judges × 3 axes + 1 partial third judge + RATE control on 1 cell. Multi-benchmark, scaled replication, and alternative rewriter experiments were not done. Assess whether the executed experiments adequately support the claims as scoped.\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods + Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n\n### Missing Experiments / Desired Ablations\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the scale)\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards with ~21% Main acceptance rate.","uuid":"6a9f8b63-cb70-4ec7-ae36-2576c84ac419","session_id":"c31a7728-a7db-4599-bbbd-5af253d1fc6a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **methodology soundness** and **experimental rigor**. Produce an independent review — do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\nScoring scale (Overall Assessment, half-point increments):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (Main)\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Calibrate accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Core claim:** Standard Likert scoring systematically fails to detect style bias in LLM judges (\"Likert Blindspot\"), while pairwise comparison on counterfactual pairs reveals significant preferences. The paper introduces the CSD (Counterfactual Style Decomposition) pipeline.\n\n**Three claims:**\n- C1: CSD detects significant style bias in 5/6 primary judge×axis cells (BT 63–77%, p<0.001)\n- C2: Likert Blindspot confirmed in 5/6 cells — Likert ATE NS (p: 0.179–0.908) where pairwise is significant\n- C3: GEE decomposition reveals cross-axis confound gradient: verbosity=pure style, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated\n\n**Method (CSD pipeline):**\n1. Counterfactual generation via Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct rewriter (binary style pairs)\n2. NLI verification gate (bidirectional entailment; formality 92% pass, register 94%, tone excluded at 65%)\n3. Position-controlled pairwise scoring (both orderings, 200 judgments per 100 pairs)\n4. Bradley-Terry model fitting with bootstrap CIs (10K resamples)\n5. GEE confound decomposition: logit(P(win)) = β_style·𝟙[style+] + β_orig·𝟙[original]\n\n**Experimental setup:**\n- Benchmark: LLMBar only (100 instruction-output pairs)\n- Judges: GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B (primary); Claude-3.5-Sonnet (formality only, directional)\n- Axes: formality, verbosity, register (tone excluded)\n- n=100 pairs per cell\n- RATE control on GPT-4o formality only\n- Holm-Bonferroni correction for 6 primary cells; GEE reported with nominal p-values\n\n**Key results:**\n- Table 1 (2×3 evidence matrix): Formality BT 63–66.5%, Verbosity BT 77–78%, Register GPT-4o 58% NS / Qwen GEE 1.91*\n- Table 2 (Likert vs Pairwise): All 7 cells Likert NS, 6/7 pairwise significant\n- Table 3 (GEE): Verbosity pure style (OR 10.5–13.1), formality mixed, register originality-dominated\n- Table 4 (RATE control): Formal style BT 63% sig vs original pref BT 55.5% NS\n- Claude-3.5 formality: BT 62% p=0.005, Likert p=0.42\n\n**What experiments were planned but NOT executed (from review context):**\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark (AlpacaEval/MT-Bench) — not executed, budget exhausted\n- exp-006: Scaled replication n=300-500 — not executed\n- exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control — not executed (P2 priority)\n- exp-001: Third judge (Claude-3.5) only completed formality, Gemini incomplete\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness** — Is the CSD pipeline well-designed? Are the statistical methods appropriate? Is the GEE model correctly specified? Are assumptions stated and reasonable? Any flaws in the causal reasoning?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor** — Is the evidence sufficient for the claims? Are there missing controls or ablations? Is n=100 adequate? Is the single-benchmark limitation serious? Is the single-rewriter a threat to validity? How complete is the evidence matrix?\n\n3. **Statistical Concerns** — Are the corrections appropriate? Is the BT model properly applied? Are CIs reliable at this sample size? Is the \"Blindspot\" argument circular (comparing two methods that by construction have different power)?\n\n4. **Reproducibility** — Can this be replicated? Are details sufficient?\n\n5. **Coverage vs. Plan** — The paper covers 2 primary judges × 3 axes + 1 partial third judge + RATE control on 1 cell. Multi-benchmark, scaled replication, and alternative rewriter experiments were not done. Assess whether the executed experiments adequately support the claims as scoped.\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods + Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n\n### Missing Experiments / Desired Ablations\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the scale)\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards with ~21% Main acceptance rate."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01En36V24Y5wN7AVuRMBbnW2","session_id":"c31a7728-a7db-4599-bbbd-5af253d1fc6a","uuid":"91bd9bdb-e7bd-4d51-8ea4-ed92cbc7749f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:59:11.887Z"}
