ylistic)\n\n3. **Measurement Sensitivity:** DeCoster et al. 2011, Feinstein 1990, Stevens 1946 — psychometric literature on forced-choice vs Likert\n\n4. **Bradley-Terry Models:** Bradley and Terry 1952, Zheng et al. 2024 (Chatbot Arena)\n\n**Method:** CSD pipeline = counterfactual generation → NLI verification → position-controlled pairwise scoring → Bradley-Terry → GEE confound decomposition\n\n**Key novelty claims:**\n- \"Likert Blindspot\" phenomenon named and characterized\n- CSD pipeline combining counterfactual + pairwise + GEE decomposition\n- Cross-axis confound gradient (verbosity=pure, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated)\n- Critique of RATE's reliance on Likert ATE\n\n**Setup:** LLMBar benchmark only, 2 primary judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B), 3 axes, n=100 per cell\n\n**What was NOT done:** multi-benchmark, alternative rewriter, scaled n, full third judge coverage\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment** — How novel is the \"Likert Blindspot\" observation? The psychometric literature already knows forced-choice > Likert for moderate effects. Is naming this phenomenon in the LLM domain a sufficient contribution? Is the CSD pipeline novel or an assembly of known components (counterfactuals + BT + GEE)? Is the GEE gradient finding genuinely new?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness** — Are there missing related works? Is the positioning against RATE fair and accurate? Is the framing against CASSE/SOS-Bench appropriate? Are there other counterfactual or pairwise approaches that should be cited?\n\n3. **Contribution Significance** — Would this change how practitioners audit LLM judges? Is the practical impact meaningful? Does the single-benchmark, 2-judge scope limit the significance?\n\n4. **Framing and Claims** — Are claims appropriately scoped (\"across the two judges tested\")? Is the paper overselling or underselling? Is \"Blindspot\" the right framing or is it just a power difference between measurement methods?\n\n5. **Writing Quality** — Is the paper well-organized? Are the tables/figures effective? Is the 7-page length appropriate?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty + Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n\n### Missing Related Work\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the scale)\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards with ~21% Main acceptance rate. Consider that Findings track requires solid contribution but lower novelty bar than Main.","uuid":"879e5d34-be5f-4715-b843-ebba1d8986dd","session_id":"c31a7728-a7db-4599-bbbd-5af253d1fc6a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **novelty/originality** and **positioning relative to related work**. Produce an independent review — do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\nScoring scale (Overall Assessment, half-point increments):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (Main)\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Calibrate accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Core claim:** Standard Likert scoring systematically fails to detect style bias in LLM judges (\"Likert Blindspot\"), while pairwise comparison on counterfactual pairs reveals significant preferences. The paper introduces the CSD (Counterfactual Style Decomposition) pipeline.\n\n**Three claims:**\n- C1: CSD detects significant style bias in 5/6 primary judge×axis cells (BT 63–77%, p<0.001)\n- C2: Likert Blindspot confirmed in 5/6 cells — Likert ATE NS (p: 0.179–0.908) where pairwise is significant\n- C3: GEE decomposition reveals cross-axis confound gradient: verbosity=pure style, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated\n\n**Related Work as presented in paper (Section 2):**\n\n1. **Style Bias in LLM Judges:** Wu and Aji 2024 (EMNLP), LC-AlpacaEval (Dubois et al. 2024b), SOS-Bench (Ye et al. 2024 — correlational), CASSE (Song et al. 2024 — 12×12 matrix but no disentangling)\n\n2. **Counterfactual Approaches:** RATE (Chen et al. 2025, ICML — double-rewrite + Likert ATE), Counterfactual Rhetorical (Zhou et al. 2025 — persona-based + BT, but rhetorical not stylistic)\n\n3. **Measurement Sensitivity:** DeCoster et al. 2011, Feinstein 1990, Stevens 1946 — psychometric literature on forced-choice vs Likert\n\n4. **Bradley-Terry Models:** Bradley and Terry 1952, Zheng et al. 2024 (Chatbot Arena)\n\n**Method:** CSD pipeline = counterfactual generation → NLI verification → position-controlled pairwise scoring → Bradley-Terry → GEE confound decomposition\n\n**Key novelty claims:**\n- \"Likert Blindspot\" phenomenon named and characterized\n- CSD pipeline combining counterfactual + pairwise + GEE decomposition\n- Cross-axis confound gradient (verbosity=pure, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated)\n- Critique of RATE's reliance on Likert ATE\n\n**Setup:** LLMBar benchmark only, 2 primary judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B), 3 axes, n=100 per cell\n\n**What was NOT done:** multi-benchmark, alternative rewriter, scaled n, full third judge coverage\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment** — How novel is the \"Likert Blindspot\" observation? The psychometric literature already knows forced-choice > Likert for moderate effects. Is naming this phenomenon in the LLM domain a sufficient contribution? Is the CSD pipeline novel or an assembly of known components (counterfactuals + BT + GEE)? Is the GEE gradient finding genuinely new?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness** — Are there missing related works? Is the positioning against RATE fair and accurate? Is the framing against CASSE/SOS-Bench appropriate? Are there other counterfactual or pairwise approaches that should be cited?\n\n3. **Contribution Significance** — Would this change how practitioners audit LLM judges? Is the practical impact meaningful? Does the single-benchmark, 2-judge scope limit the significance?\n\n4. **Framing and Claims** — Are claims appropriately scoped (\"across the two judges tested\")? Is the paper overselling or underselling? Is \"Blindspot\" the right framing or is it just a power difference between measurement methods?\n\n5. **Writing Quality** — Is the paper well-organized? Are the tables/figures effective? Is the 7-page length appropriate?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty + Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n\n### Missing Related Work\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the scale)\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards with ~21% Main acceptance rate. Consider that Findings track requires solid contribution but lower novelty bar than Main."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SBCWDvNA8XB72AGMszUU15","session_id":"c31a7728-a7db-4599-bbbd-5af253d1fc6a","uuid":"816e7dab-0d5f-4246-b440-92326ade8586","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:59:35.498Z"}
