{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013Eeg9jc1u1T3ZQvUfnBPwD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Method}\n2\t\\label{sec:method}\n3\t\n4\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n5\t\\centering\n6\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/pipeline_diagram.pdf}\n7\t\\caption{CSD pipeline overview. Numbers indicate sample attrition at each stage.}\n8\t\\label{fig:pipeline}\n9\t\\end{figure}\n10\t\n11\t\\subsection{Style-Controlled Rewriting}\n12\t\\label{sec:rewriting}\n13\t\n14\tWe isolate the effect of individual style attributes on judge scores by generating text pairs that differ along exactly one style axis.\n15\tFor each text, an instruction-tuned LLM (Qwen3-32B) first classifies its current style along the target axis (e.g., formal vs.\\ casual via binary classification), then rewrites it toward the opposite pole (temperature 0.7) according to an axis-specific prompt (e.g., ``Rewrite the following text to be more formal/casual while preserving the exact same content and meaning'').\n16\tThis produces a dataset balanced across both directions at the dataset level: texts detected as formal are rewritten to casual, and vice versa.\n17\tEach operation constitutes a binary intervention on a single style variable while all other axes remain fixed (Section~\\ref{sec:causal_framework}).\n18\tTo control for rewriting artifact bias---the possibility that the judge prefers original text over any rewrite regardless of style---we additionally generate a same-style double-rewrite for each text (e.g., formal $\\to$ formal) to form an original-vs-double-rewrite control pair, following the RATE bias correction protocol \\citep{reber2025rate}.\n19\t\n20\t\\subsection{Content Preservation Verification}\n21\t\\label{sec:nli_gate}\n22\t\n23\tStyle-controlled rewriting risks altering semantic content, which would confound the style effect with a content quality effect (the $R \\to Q$ path in Figure~\\ref{fig:dag}).\n24\tTo block this path, we apply a natural language inference (NLI) gate using DeBERTa-v3-large with an entailment threshold of 0.90.\n25\tFor axes that preserve information density (formality, register), we apply standard bidirectional entailment: both NLI(original $\\to$ rewrite) and NLI(rewrite $\\to$ original) must exceed this value.\n26\tFor axes that inherently change information density (e.g., verbosity), bidirectional verification is inappropriate---expansion necessarily introduces new content that the original cannot entail.\n27\tWe instead apply direction-aware verification: for expansions, only backward entailment (rewrite entails original); for compressions, only forward entailment (original entails rewrite).\n28\tThis recovered verbosity from 65\\% to 86\\% pass rate.\n29\tThis gating mechanism approximates the \\emph{do}-operator \\citep{veitch2020adapting} by ensuring that retained pairs differ only in style, not in content quality.\n30\tWe additionally compute BERTScore as a supplementary monitoring metric, but it does not participate in the gating decision.\n31\t\n32\t\\subsection{Measurement Protocol}\n33\t\\label{sec:measurement}\n34\t\n35\tEach pair that passes the NLI gate is evaluated by GPT-4o (\\texttt{gpt-4o-2024-08-06}) under two complementary scoring modes.\n36\tIn Likert mode, the judge assigns an integer score from 1 to 10 for overall quality to each text independently.\n37\tIn pairwise mode, the judge receives both texts as candidates A and B and makes a forced choice of which response is better.\n38\t\n39\tTo control for position bias, each pairwise comparison is evaluated twice with the presentation order swapped, which produces $n_{\\text{pairs}} \\times 2$ observations.\n40\tWe apply two analysis strategies to these data.\n41\tFor the nonparametric sign test, we retain only \\emph{consistent} pairs where the judge selected the same text in both orderings.\n42\tFor the Bradley-Terry model, we use all observations including inconsistent pairs, as the model naturally accommodates noise in individual comparisons.\n43\t\n44\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n45\t\\label{sec:setup}\n46\t\n47\tWe evaluate on LLMBar \\citep{zeng2023llmbar}, a benchmark of $n = 100$ instruction-response pairs spanning diverse task types, designed to test LLM judges' ability to distinguish response quality with human-verified quality labels.\n48\tLLMBar is well-suited for style bias detection because its human-verified labels provide ground truth against which judge deviations can be measured, and its moderate size permits exhaustive pairwise evaluation with position control.\n49\t\n50\t\\subsection{Causal Framework}\n51\t\\label{sec:causal_framework}\n52\t\n53\tFigure~\\ref{fig:dag} presents the assumed data-generating process.\n54\tThree variables govern the observed judge score $Y$: the style attribute $S$ (e.g., formality), the original content quality $Q$, and the judge's internal scoring function.\n55\tBoth $S$ and $Q$ causally influence $Y$; the goal of CSD is to isolate the effect $S \\to Y$ while blocking confounding through $Q$.\n56\tStyle-controlled rewriting generates a text pair that differs along a single style axis, but any rewriting operation risks inadvertently altering content quality ($R \\to Q$).\n57\tThe NLI entailment gate approximates the \\emph{do}-operator by filtering out pairs where the rewrite changed semantic content, thereby blocking the $R \\to Q$ path and yielding an interventional estimate of the $S \\to Y$ effect.\n58\tOur approach provides interventional rather than formal causal evidence: we do not claim that this DAG is a complete structural causal model, nor do we invoke SUTVA or consistency assumptions from the potential outcomes framework.\n59\t\n60\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n61\t\\centering\n62\t\\begin{tikzpicture}[\n63\t    node distance=2.2cm and 2.5cm,\n64\t    every node/.style={font=\\small},\n65\t    obs/.style={draw, circle, minimum size=1cm, thick},\n66\t    mech/.style={draw, rectangle, rounded corners, minimum size=0.8cm, thick, fill=gray!10},\n67\t    gate/.style={draw, rectangle, dashed, thick, fill=blue!8, font=\\footnotesize},\n68\t    arr/.style={-{Stealth[length=5pt]}, thick},\n69\t    blocked/.style={-{Stealth[length=5pt]}, thick, dashed, red!60},\n70\t  ]\n71\t  \\node[obs] (S) {$S$};\n72\t  \\node[obs, right=of S] (Y) {$Y$};\n73\t  \\node[obs, above=1.5cm of Y] (Q) {$Q$};\n74\t  \\node[mech, left=1.8cm of Q] (R) {Rewrite};\n75\t  \\node[gate, below=0.4cm of R] (NLI) {NLI gate};\n76\t\n77\t  \\draw[arr] (S) -- node[below, font=\\footnotesize] {style bias} (Y);\n78\t  \\draw[arr] (Q) -- node[right, font=\\footnotesize] {quality} (Y);\n79\t  \\draw[arr] (R) -- node[above, font=\\footnotesize] {} (S);\n80\t  \\draw[blocked] (R) -- node[above, font=\\footnotesize, black] {\\texttimes} (Q);\n81\t\n82\t  \\draw[arr, gray] (NLI) -- (R);\n83\t\n84\t\\end{tikzpicture}\n85\t\\caption{Assumed data-generating process. The rewriting mechanism $R$ targets style $S$ but may inadvertently alter content quality $Q$ (dashed red arrow). The NLI entailment gate filters pairs where $Q$ changed, approximating an intervention on $S$ alone. $Y$: judge score.}\n86\t\\label{fig:dag}\n87\t\\end{figure}\n88\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01En36V24Y5wN7AVuRMBbnW2","session_id":"c31a7728-a7db-4599-bbbd-5af253d1fc6a","uuid":"3cc52800-5f53-4db1-afa2-859ad2c53c26","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:59:41.729Z"}
