{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BwaPmgLqkUH28kAvog3UmR","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/main.bbl:99:\\newblock \\href {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11348} {{RATE}: Causal\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:7:\\citet{reber2025rate} propose RATE, a framework that rewrites model outputs along style attributes using imperfect counterfactuals and measures the causal effect on reward model scores via Likert-scale Average Treatment Effect (ATE).\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:8:RATE applies a correction term to account for rewriting imperfections, but its reliance on Likert ATE as the primary metric is a vulnerability: our experiments show that Likert scoring exhibits a ceiling effect in which 60\\% of style-transformed pairs receive identical integer scores, yielding non-significant ATE estimates ($p = 0.332$) for the same interventions that pairwise comparison detects at $p = 0.006$.\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:9:If this Likert Blindspot generalizes beyond our setting, RATE's own style-related findings may underestimate the true magnitude of attribute effects.\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:10:CSD differs from RATE in three respects: it targets individual style axes rather than general attributes, it measures bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison rather than Likert ATE, and it enforces content preservation via NLI entailment gating rather than post-hoc correction for imperfect rewrites.\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:76:\\paragraph{Head-to-head comparison with RATE.}\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:77:Applying RATE's Likert ATE methodology \\citep{reber2025rate} to our formality style-transformed pairs yields Cohen's $d = 0.10$, $p = 0.33$ (non-significant, $n = 88$); pairwise comparison on identical data reveals a 63.0\\% formal preference ($p < 0.001$, Bradley-Terry on all 173 comparisons), confirmed by a consistency-filtered sign test (73.9\\%, $p = 0.006$, $n = 46$ consistent pairs).\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:81:RATE measures aggregate attribute-level quality effects via Likert ATE and is designed to detect mean score shifts on continuous rating scales.\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:126:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/sections/conclusion.tex:10:Existing Likert-based results, including those from RATE and similar frameworks, may need reexamination under pairwise measurement to assess whether reported null findings reflect genuine absence of bias or measurement insensitivity.\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:18:To control for rewriting artifact bias---the possibility that the judge prefers original text over any rewrite regardless of style---we additionally generate a same-style double-rewrite for each text (e.g., formal $\\to$ formal) to form an original-vs-double-rewrite control pair, following the RATE bias correction protocol \\citep{reber2025rate}.\ndocs/paper/main.aux:510:\\@writefile{toc}{\\contentsline {paragraph}{Head-to-head comparison with RATE.}{5}{section*.11}\\protected@file@percent }\ndocs/paper/main.aux:617:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/main.aux:618:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/acl_natbib.bst:1848:ITERATE {presort}\ndocs/paper/acl_natbib.bst:1901:ITERATE {forward.pass}\ndocs/paper/acl_natbib.bst:1917:ITERATE {bib.sort.order}\ndocs/paper/acl_natbib.bst:1932:ITERATE {call.type$}\ndocs/paper/references.bib:59:  title={{RATE}: Causal Explainability of Reward Models with Imperfect Counterfactuals},"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01En36V24Y5wN7AVuRMBbnW2","session_id":"c31a7728-a7db-4599-bbbd-5af253d1fc6a","uuid":"4ae76c19-c364-437a-985b-5cf01f24ea40","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:00:09.589Z"}
